+**The port has a blind spot, and it is worth knowing before you trust it.** The
+assertion is a regex over workflow YAML, so it proves only that no workflow
+*file* contains an inline `git commit`/`git push`. It cannot see a git write
+inside a `uses:` action. Measured 2026-08-23 against a repo used as a fixture:
+the ratchet ran clean — 4 tests, 0 failures — while 92 of 116 commits on `main`
+reported `verified: false`, 71 of them from a third-party action invoked by a
+workflow that contains no git command at all. `reason: unsigned` is what settles
+it: `createCommitOnBranch` would have produced *verified* bot commits, so that
+action writes history with `git` in the runner.
+
+`registry-refresh.yml` inlines its git write, so the ratchet does catch that
+instance — it does not close the class. Treating a green run as "this repo cannot
+produce unsigned commits" would be the same inference this document exists to
+warn about.
+
+The check that closes the class asserts the **outcome** rather than the
+mechanism: every commit on the ref reports `verification.verified`. Whatever
+wrote it, by whatever route, GitHub either verified it or did not. Build it to
+fail closed on every transport failure — non-2xx, missing token, empty scan,
+truncated pagination — for exactly the §2 reason: a check that reads its own
+failed read as "nothing to report" is a green light generated by the absence of
+information. A working implementation, mutation-tested, is staged at
+`bdelanghe/bdelanghe:handoff/github-private-690-part3/`.
+
