+  **Stronger than first written (2026-08-23):** configuring
+  `gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile`, as the sentence above implies you should, makes
+  `%G?` report `B` — *bad signature* — not `N`. Git routes verification through
+  `gpg.ssh.program` too, and that broker is sign-only
+  (`unsupported code-sign operation: currently only SSH-style signing (-Y sign)
+  is supported`); git renders the refusal as a bad signature. There is no
+  `ssh-keygen` in the image to substitute. `B` reads far more like "you broke
+  signing" than `N` does, so this trap gets *worse* for a session that follows
+  the advice. The safe rule is absolute: **no `%G?` value in this environment
+  carries information.** Use `git cat-file`, or verify the blob yourself — the
+  SSHSIG payload is `"SSHSIG" || namespace || reserved || hash_alg || H(commit)`,
+  which is ~30 lines of `node:crypto` and settles it locally. Commits made this
+  way were confirmed `verified: true, reason: valid` on GitHub while `%G?` said
+  `B`.
